{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>They turned aside quickly from the way.</b> It is explained according to the words of the Sages (Ḥullin 5a) who said: one who acknowledges idolatry is as one who denies the entire Torah in its entirety. And that is what the verse says — <b>they turned aside from the way which I commanded them</b> — behold, everything He commanded is included." "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to what we explained, that Israel were still in complete faith in HaShem, except they thought to have before them a certain power from El Elyon. Therefore He said <b>they turned aside from the way which I commanded them</b> — meaning, from not making intermediaries, and they made a way of intermediaries. And for this reason He did not say 'they rebelled against Me' or 'they denied Me,' for they were still in their faith." "<b>They made for themselves, etc.</b> The meaning is: others made the calf for them, for they gave the gold and the calf emerged on its own. And HaShem informed that it did not emerge on its own, as Aaron said (Exodus 32:24): ״וַיֵּצֵא הָעֵגֶל הַזֶּה״ — but rather through an act. And this is the statement of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Tanchuma) that Yonus and Yimbrus made, etc." "<b>And they said: These, etc.</b> It is difficult: why did He reverse the order of what was done? He should have placed the saying of ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ before the sacrificing, for so we find that on the first day they said ״אֵלֶּה״ etc., and on the morrow they sacrificed etc. Now, according to the words of the Sages of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 42:6) that the mixed multitude were the ones who performed the entire act of the calf and not Israel, Scripture arranges the gradations of the sin according to the reckoning of Israel, not according to the reckoning of the mixed multitude. And He states one gradation: <b>they made for themselves</b> — meaning, the mixed multitude made a molten calf for themselves and Israel did not protest. Further, the mixed multitude bowed down and sacrificed and Israel did not protest, and this is a greater gradation than the first. And further, they said explicitly ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ יִשְׂרָאֵל״ and Israel remained silent — and this is the harshest of them all." "And according to the plain sense of Scripture, the reason that He delayed their saying of ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ — although it is in the aspect of blemish of speech and is an intermediate gradation between thought and deed — is that since there is in it the aspect of speech, this gradation is great, for they accepted it as a god for all time in their saying ״אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱלוּךָ״. And this reason indicates the persistence to future generations, and it is a wondrous blemish; therefore He placed it last, in the manner of ״לֹא זוֹ אַף זוֹ״ ('not only this, but even this')." "<b>Further</b>, it appears that the reason is based on what the meaning of ״אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ conveys when addressed to them — that it was not Israel who were speaking. If so, the matter hangs in the balance: whether those who heard the statement ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ agreed to this or not. Therefore at first it said only ״עָשׂוּ לָהֶם עֵגֶל״, and after they bowed and sacrificed to it, it was revealed retroactively that they heard and agreed to the matter. And therefore after HaShem mentioned ״וַיִּשְׁתַּחֲווּ וַיִּזְבְּחוּ לוֹ״, He said <b>וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֵלֶּה</b> etc. — to say that the proof that they agreed is from the fact that they bowed etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:35:53.748217903Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}